{"name":"Elk Antler","hex":"#cabca4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"elk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Elkei."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various large species of deer such as the red deer, moose or wapiti (see usage notes).\n Any of the subspecies of the moose (Alces alces, the largest member of the deer family, alternatively named Eurasian elk to avoid confusion with the wapiti), that occurs only in Europe and Asia.\n Common wapiti (Cervus canadensis), the second largest member of the deer family, once thought to be a subspecies of red deer.\n Sambar (Cervus unicolor).","Any of the subspecies of the moose (Alces alces, the largest member of the deer family, alternatively named Eurasian elk to avoid confusion with the wapiti), that occurs only in Europe and Asia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elk"},{"term":"antler","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous branching and bony structure on the head of deer (Cervidae), normally in pairs, grown and shed each year."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/antler"}],"entries":[{"title":"Elk","blocks":[{"text":"The elk or wapiti is the second largest species within the deer family, Cervidae, and one of the largest terrestrial mammals in its native range of North America and Central and East Asia. The word \"elk\" originally referred to the European variety of the moose, Alces alces, but was transferred to Cervus canadensis by North American colonists."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Jasper.Wapiti-Hirsch.P1033401.jpg/330px-Jasper.Wapiti-Hirsch.P1033401.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elk"},{"title":"Antler","blocks":[{"text":"Antlers are extensions of an animal's skull found in members of the Cervidae (deer) family. Antlers are a single structure composed of bone, cartilage, fibrous tissue, skin, nerves, and blood vessels. They are generally found only on males, with the exception of reindeer/caribou. Antlers are shed and regrown each year and function primarily as objects of sexual attraction and as weapons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Red_deer_stag_2009_denmark.jpg/330px-Red_deer_stag_2009_denmark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antler"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Rocky_Mountain_elk_%28Cervus_canadensis_nelsoni%29_female_Banff.jpg/500px-Rocky_Mountain_elk_%28Cervus_canadensis_nelsoni%29_female_Banff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Rocky_Mountain_elk_%28Cervus_canadensis_nelsoni%29_female_Banff.jpg/1280px-Rocky_Mountain_elk_%28Cervus_canadensis_nelsoni%29_female_Banff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Rocky_Mountain_elk_%28Cervus_canadensis_nelsoni%29_female_Banff.jpg/500px-Rocky_Mountain_elk_%28Cervus_canadensis_nelsoni%29_female_Banff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARocky_Mountain_elk_(Cervus_canadensis_nelsoni)_female_Banff.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Elk_Bull_in_Yellowstone.jpg/500px-Elk_Bull_in_Yellowstone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Elk_Bull_in_Yellowstone.jpg/1280px-Elk_Bull_in_Yellowstone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Elk_Bull_in_Yellowstone.jpg/500px-Elk_Bull_in_Yellowstone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.